{
  "dom_id": "step-q343183",
  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14232470/assets/c410aeaa3ceeafe3b072233a51020594370431e35244ca240637c5b124807983.png)\n\nA firefighter $F$ is lowering a rescue basket $B$ from a helicopter. The basket stays $40\\,\\text{ft}$ above the ground while it moves horizontally toward the firefighter at a speed of $12\\,\\text{ft/s}.$ Find, in radians per second as a function of $\\theta ,$ the rate at which the angle $\\theta $ between the cable and the horizontal is increasing.\n\nThe angle is increasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{rad/s}.$",
  "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14232470/assets/cfac2040d258398133397c5322e650bf60f59c7c372c97bfecec654eb3a4a278.png)\n\nIn the figure, $y$ is the basket's height, and $x$ is the horizontal distance between the firefighter and the basket. The quantity $y=40$ feet does not change with time because the basket height remains the same.\n\nWe wish to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}.$ To do this, let's start by setting up a relation between $x$ and $y.$ Relative to the angle $\\theta ,$ the sides $x$ and $y$ are the adjacent and opposite, respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{y}{x} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{40}{x} \\\\ x & =40\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(40\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =(-40{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given information, we have $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=-12\\,\\text{ft/s}$ (negative because the horizontal distance is decreasing). Substituting this into the above, we reach\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-12 & =(-40{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{12}{40} & =({csc}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{12}{40}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{3}{10}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the angle is increasing at a rate of $\\frac{3}{10}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{rad/s}.$",
